      "costUsd": 0.0076895,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's a list of numbers: minus one, zero, nine, thirty-two. They're the positions of a moving particle, read at one, two, three and four seconds. A list of numbers in a fixed order, like this, is called a sequence. Sequences are everywhere. At a cricket match, read the scoreboard at the end of each over. The runs scored in each over make one sequence, and those end-of-over totals make another. So what exactly is a sequence, and how do we add up its terms neatly?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f01/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1479,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.8430000000000035,
        "after": "seven.",
        "before": "The"
      },
      "cues": [
        {
          "text": "five",
          "start": 19.203000000000053,
          "end": 19.543000000000053
        },
        {
          "text": "one",
          "start": 34.4020000000001,
          "end": 34.4777500000001
        },
        {
          "text": "seven",
          "start": 41.633999999999915,
          "end": 41.86816666666659
        }
      ],
      "seconds": 49.28,
      "peakFraction": 0.84539794921875,
      "fullScaleSamples": 0,
      "silencesAtLeast1_5s": [],
      "lowScoreWords": [],
      "successfulRequestCount": 1,
      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 171,
      "outputTokens": 1232,
      "costUsd": 0.011173500000000001,
      "pronunciationText": "A sequence is an ordered list of objects, usually numbers. Each object is called a term, and every term has a position: first, second, third, and so on. We write a sub n for the term in position n, where n is a positive whole number. Say one team scores one, three, five and seven runs in the first four overs. Here, a sub three is five. The three is the position, the third over, and the five is the value, the runs. Now a second team scores the same runs in reverse: seven, five, three, one. After four overs, both teams have sixteen. But after the first over, one scoreboard shows one, and the other shows seven. The positions stay put and the values move. That's why order matters, and why these are different sequences."
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f01/scene-03.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1481,
      "worstWordGap": {
