      "loggedRequestAttempts": 1,
      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 159,
      "outputTokens": 1234,
      "costUsd": 0.0111855,
      "pronunciationText": "Often a rule gives each term from its position. Say a team scores two n runs in over n. Put in n equals one, and a sub one is two. Then come four, six, eight and ten. The rule only needs the position. So to find a sub fifty, put in fifty and get one hundred, without listing the first forty-nine terms. If the game stops after five overs, the list stops at ten, its fifth term, so it's a finite sequence. A finite sequence ends at some last term, a sub m. Here m is five. But if the list never stops, two, four, six, eight, ten, and so on forever, it's an infinite sequence. We show that with three dots at the end."
    },
    {
