      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 109,
      "outputTokens": 760,
      "costUsd": 0.0068945,
      "pronunciationText": "Let's go the other way. A sequence has a sub n equals n squared minus n plus one. Its first five terms are one, three, seven, thirteen and twenty-one. Their sum, S five, is forty-five. To write S five in sigma notation, swap n for the counter k, start at one and stop at five. So S five is the sum, from k equals one to five, of k squared minus k plus one. That's forty-five again, written in one line."
    },
